WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.870 --> 00:00:03.600
GRACE: Dr. Lorraine Murphy
is an associate professor

00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:06.510
of English at Hillsdale
College, where she has

00:00:06.510 --> 00:00:09.150
taught for the past 10 years.

00:00:09.150 --> 00:00:11.940
A recipient of the Emily
Daugherty Award for Teaching

00:00:11.940 --> 00:00:16.500
Excellence, she teaches courses
in realism, the 18th century

00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:19.660
novel, and Jane Austen.

00:00:19.660 --> 00:00:23.590
She serves as faculty advisor to
the Cravats and Blue Stockings

00:00:23.590 --> 00:00:28.690
Regency Appreciation Club and as
secretary for the Phi Kappa Phi

00:00:28.690 --> 00:00:30.760
Honor Society.

00:00:30.760 --> 00:00:35.020
She taught a Hillsdale College
online course titled "The Young

00:00:35.020 --> 00:00:38.050
Jane Austen, Northanger
Abbey," which

00:00:38.050 --> 00:00:42.040
is available at
online.Hillsdale.edu.

00:00:42.040 --> 00:00:44.260
Please join me in
welcoming Dr. Murphy.

00:00:44.260 --> 00:00:47.704
[APPLAUSE]

00:00:56.580 --> 00:00:58.240
LORRAINE MURPHY:
Thank you, Grace.

00:00:58.240 --> 00:01:00.660
And I'd also like to thank
Doug Jeffrey and Matt

00:01:00.660 --> 00:01:04.319
Bell for inviting me to speak
and all of you for being here

00:01:04.319 --> 00:01:07.080
to listen.

00:01:07.080 --> 00:01:10.110
That her novels have been so
repeatedly and successfully

00:01:10.110 --> 00:01:13.680
adapted to film tells us
something of Austen's skill

00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:16.320
in scripting plot and character.

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:19.470
But one thing a film cannot
give you is the voice

00:01:19.470 --> 00:01:21.450
of the narrator.

00:01:21.450 --> 00:01:24.780
I'd like to open tonight
with a passage from Emma

00:01:24.780 --> 00:01:27.780
that I believe reveals something
essential about Austen's

00:01:27.780 --> 00:01:30.330
particular genius.

00:01:30.330 --> 00:01:34.050
Emma, in this passage, has
accompanied her protege Harriet

00:01:34.050 --> 00:01:35.745
to the general
store in Highbury.

00:01:38.270 --> 00:01:42.050
"Harriet, tempted by everything
and swayed by half a word,

00:01:42.050 --> 00:01:44.450
was always very
long at a purchase.

00:01:44.450 --> 00:01:46.940
And while she was still
hanging over muslins

00:01:46.940 --> 00:01:51.890
and changing her mind, Emma
went to the door for amusement.

00:01:51.890 --> 00:01:55.160
Much could not be hoped from
the traffic of even the busiest

00:01:55.160 --> 00:01:56.690
part of Highbury--

00:01:56.690 --> 00:02:01.250
Mr. Perry walking hastily by,
Mr. William Cox letting himself

00:02:01.250 --> 00:02:04.970
in at the office door,
Mr. Cole's carriage horses

00:02:04.970 --> 00:02:08.360
returning from exercise,
or a stray letter boy

00:02:08.360 --> 00:02:12.050
on an obstinate mule were
the liveliest objects

00:02:12.050 --> 00:02:14.660
she could presume to expect.

00:02:14.660 --> 00:02:17.540
And when her eyes fell
only on the butcher

00:02:17.540 --> 00:02:20.990
with his tray, a tidy old
woman traveling homewards

00:02:20.990 --> 00:02:24.740
from shop with her full
basket, two curs quarreling

00:02:24.740 --> 00:02:27.170
over a dirty bone, and
a string of dawdling

00:02:27.170 --> 00:02:29.840
children around the baker's
little bow window eyeing

00:02:29.840 --> 00:02:33.530
the gingerbread, she knew
she had no reason to complain

00:02:33.530 --> 00:02:36.770
and was amused enough,
quite enough still

00:02:36.770 --> 00:02:39.070
to stand at the door.

00:02:39.070 --> 00:02:43.760
A mind lively and at ease
can do with seeing nothing

00:02:43.760 --> 00:02:48.310
and can see nothing
that does not answer."

00:02:48.310 --> 00:02:52.540
This, I suggest, is the
secret to Austen's art.

00:02:52.540 --> 00:02:56.410
Gratefully secure in a mundane
setting where others might

00:02:56.410 --> 00:02:59.740
see nothing of
interest, Jane Austen

00:02:59.740 --> 00:03:02.470
could do with seeing nothing.

00:03:02.470 --> 00:03:05.170
Her lively mind could
discern and articulate

00:03:05.170 --> 00:03:08.080
the meaning in a series
of commonplace actions

00:03:08.080 --> 00:03:11.950
that would not answer to
a more callous observer.

00:03:11.950 --> 00:03:16.210
By addressing her attention to
an ordinary scene such as this,

00:03:16.210 --> 00:03:18.580
Austen gives it a voice.

00:03:18.580 --> 00:03:21.340
The most everyday materials
are rendered eloquent

00:03:21.340 --> 00:03:25.870
by her precise and
affectionate language.

00:03:25.870 --> 00:03:29.230
It's my privilege this evening
to talk about Austen's life

00:03:29.230 --> 00:03:32.560
with the aim of understanding
how it nurtured her talent.

00:03:32.560 --> 00:03:35.050
And my thesis is this--

00:03:35.050 --> 00:03:37.090
that her life and
work were fueled

00:03:37.090 --> 00:03:41.050
by a passionate or, in the
words of GK Chesterton,

00:03:41.050 --> 00:03:45.460
an "exuberant interest
in the ordinary."

00:03:45.460 --> 00:03:47.800
Jane Austen's
natural exuberance is

00:03:47.800 --> 00:03:50.290
enriched over the
course of her life

00:03:50.290 --> 00:03:52.930
by a network of
love and friendship

00:03:52.930 --> 00:03:55.900
that brings her into
contact with every variety

00:03:55.900 --> 00:04:00.940
of anticipation, disappointment,
uncertainty, loss, hope,

00:04:00.940 --> 00:04:04.000
laughter, and joy we can know.

00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:07.600
Perhaps her natural reserve
with strangers, as well as

00:04:07.600 --> 00:04:10.390
the unpredictable nature
of some relationships,

00:04:10.390 --> 00:04:13.750
encouraged that close
scrutiny of the minutia

00:04:13.750 --> 00:04:17.860
of our social behavior that
is a hallmark of her fiction

00:04:17.860 --> 00:04:21.519
and a large part of what
makes it so filmable.

00:04:21.519 --> 00:04:25.570
Her exuberant spirit is somewhat
chastened by a prolonged period

00:04:25.570 --> 00:04:28.900
of hardship and the awareness
of her dependent position

00:04:28.900 --> 00:04:30.670
in society.

00:04:30.670 --> 00:04:33.010
Yet the sense of
joyful possibility

00:04:33.010 --> 00:04:36.730
remains from first to
last ever expressed

00:04:36.730 --> 00:04:39.460
in her work with a
Mozartian fluency

00:04:39.460 --> 00:04:42.310
in the resources of
the English language.

00:04:42.310 --> 00:04:46.110
She is unfailingly precise,
and funny, and real.

00:04:49.410 --> 00:04:51.360
She was born-- excuse me--

00:04:51.360 --> 00:05:01.270
December 16, 1775, seventh of
her parents' eight children.

00:05:01.270 --> 00:05:04.060
The Austens were not
financially prosperous.

00:05:04.060 --> 00:05:07.510
But they were hardworking,
energetic, and busy.

00:05:07.510 --> 00:05:11.710
And theirs was a home where the
written word was celebrated.

00:05:11.710 --> 00:05:14.350
Jane's father, the
Reverend George Austen,

00:05:14.350 --> 00:05:17.380
served as rector of the
Church of St. Nicholas,

00:05:17.380 --> 00:05:20.710
seen here, in the small
village of Steventon, County

00:05:20.710 --> 00:05:24.950
of Hampshire,
southwest of London.

00:05:24.950 --> 00:05:29.450
Here you see the church
door framed by carvings not

00:05:29.450 --> 00:05:35.060
of saints or angels but of
a man's face and a woman's.

00:05:35.060 --> 00:05:37.700
Reverend Austen farmed
in his spare time,

00:05:37.700 --> 00:05:40.940
ran a boys boarding school
out of their crowded home,

00:05:40.940 --> 00:05:44.930
seen here, and collected a
library of some 500 volumes

00:05:44.930 --> 00:05:48.980
that Jane was free to
borrow from as she pleased.

00:05:48.980 --> 00:05:51.380
His wife Cassandra, in
addition to her duties

00:05:51.380 --> 00:05:55.340
as mother, school mistress,
head gardener, and manager

00:05:55.340 --> 00:05:57.950
of the dairy and
poultry yards, still

00:05:57.950 --> 00:06:01.640
found time to write witty
poems for special occasions.

00:06:01.640 --> 00:06:03.440
And Jane's older
brothers published

00:06:03.440 --> 00:06:06.080
a periodical and
supervised the staging of

00:06:06.080 --> 00:06:09.437
plays when family and friends
gathered at their parsonage

00:06:09.437 --> 00:06:10.145
for the holidays.

00:06:12.900 --> 00:06:14.540
The children would
have no inheritance

00:06:14.540 --> 00:06:16.730
to speak of from their parents.

00:06:16.730 --> 00:06:18.770
Indeed, Reverend
Austen and his wife

00:06:18.770 --> 00:06:20.660
spent several years
digging their way out

00:06:20.660 --> 00:06:24.290
of debt on a very small income.

00:06:24.290 --> 00:06:27.410
Their eldest son James
took a degree at Oxford

00:06:27.410 --> 00:06:30.740
and became a clergyman,
inheriting his father's post

00:06:30.740 --> 00:06:33.830
at Steventon when he retired.

00:06:33.830 --> 00:06:39.020
The next son, George, suffered
an unspecified disability,

00:06:39.020 --> 00:06:43.330
perhaps a seizure disorder
or perhaps cerebral palsy.

00:06:43.330 --> 00:06:45.170
But he lived a long
and otherwise healthy

00:06:45.170 --> 00:06:49.940
life in the care of a family
in a neighboring village.

00:06:49.940 --> 00:06:54.170
The third brother, Edward,
was adopted at the age of 12

00:06:54.170 --> 00:06:58.160
by distant relations who were
very wealthy and childless.

00:06:58.160 --> 00:07:01.160
He remained close with
his parents and siblings

00:07:01.160 --> 00:07:04.100
and came to play a pivotal
role in Jane's life.

00:07:04.100 --> 00:07:07.250
But financially and
socially, his adoption

00:07:07.250 --> 00:07:10.800
removed him to another sphere.

00:07:10.800 --> 00:07:14.340
Henry was the most gregarious
Austen and followed

00:07:14.340 --> 00:07:18.330
several professions, including
army officer, banker,

00:07:18.330 --> 00:07:22.680
and clergyman in the course
of his interesting life.

00:07:22.680 --> 00:07:25.170
Francis and Charles,
the two youngest boys,

00:07:25.170 --> 00:07:27.570
pursued careers in
the navy, Francis

00:07:27.570 --> 00:07:30.150
rising to the rank of admiral.

00:07:30.150 --> 00:07:33.870
Finally, little Cassandra
was like a second mother

00:07:33.870 --> 00:07:36.090
to her younger sister Jane.

00:07:36.090 --> 00:07:38.940
Mrs. Austen remarked,
"if Cassandra's head

00:07:38.940 --> 00:07:40.800
had been going to
be cut off, Jane

00:07:40.800 --> 00:07:42.180
would have hers cut off too."

00:07:45.400 --> 00:07:49.210
Jane's formal schooling was
brief and perhaps blessedly so

00:07:49.210 --> 00:07:52.690
as girls' schools could be a
breeding ground for infections

00:07:52.690 --> 00:07:54.400
that were too often fatal.

00:07:54.400 --> 00:07:57.100
She made a narrow escape
from a serious illness

00:07:57.100 --> 00:08:00.950
after being sent away to
school at the age of seven.

00:08:00.950 --> 00:08:03.610
Returned safely home,
she mastered needlework,

00:08:03.610 --> 00:08:06.220
which was a vital part
of her contribution

00:08:06.220 --> 00:08:09.970
to the household economy,
and enjoyed piano.

00:08:09.970 --> 00:08:13.090
Cassandra studied drawing.

00:08:13.090 --> 00:08:17.140
Her father must have encouraged
Jane to enjoy his library.

00:08:17.140 --> 00:08:19.870
And her brothers must have
brought new publications home

00:08:19.870 --> 00:08:24.190
on a regular basis for by the
time she began writing fiction

00:08:24.190 --> 00:08:27.910
at 12 or 13, she was
familiar with the classics

00:08:27.910 --> 00:08:32.140
of the earlier 18th century,
the essays of Samuel Johnson

00:08:32.140 --> 00:08:35.799
and the novels of
Samuel Richardson,

00:08:35.799 --> 00:08:39.130
as well as more contemporary
novels by Charlotte

00:08:39.130 --> 00:08:43.900
Lennox, Fanny Burney, Maria
Edgeworth, and Ann Radcliffe.

00:08:47.170 --> 00:08:49.090
Three leatherbound
notebooks were

00:08:49.090 --> 00:08:51.760
her first prized possessions.

00:08:51.760 --> 00:08:55.460
The second is inscribed
"a gift from my father."

00:08:55.460 --> 00:08:58.090
And the third is inscribed in
the hand of Reverend Austen

00:08:58.090 --> 00:09:01.420
himself, who praises
his daughter's stories

00:09:01.420 --> 00:09:07.390
as "effusions of fancy by a very
young lady consisting of tales

00:09:07.390 --> 00:09:11.470
in a style entirely new."

00:09:11.470 --> 00:09:14.410
One of the longer stories
in the second notebook

00:09:14.410 --> 00:09:18.700
is dated June 1790,
when Jane was 14.

00:09:18.700 --> 00:09:22.660
Her first notebook was
by then already full.

00:09:22.660 --> 00:09:25.750
Her practice was to
draft on scrap paper

00:09:25.750 --> 00:09:28.390
and then transcribe her
stories into the notebooks

00:09:28.390 --> 00:09:32.590
when she considered them ready,
so to speak, for publication.

00:09:32.590 --> 00:09:35.590
Individual stories were
dedicated to various friends

00:09:35.590 --> 00:09:37.340
and family members.

00:09:37.340 --> 00:09:40.980
And the notebooks were passed
around in the Austen circle

00:09:40.980 --> 00:09:43.510
in the manner of
published books.

00:09:43.510 --> 00:09:45.400
It sounds very
serious, and it is.

00:09:45.400 --> 00:09:48.970
But the stories themselves
are full of hilarity.

00:09:48.970 --> 00:09:51.250
Young Jane delights in satire.

00:09:51.250 --> 00:09:53.650
And in particular,
she enjoys sending up

00:09:53.650 --> 00:09:56.410
the emotional excess
that was characteristic

00:09:56.410 --> 00:09:59.710
of popular fiction at the time.

00:09:59.710 --> 00:10:02.110
In a story in the
epistolary style composed

00:10:02.110 --> 00:10:06.070
as a series of letters
that Jane wrote at 14,

00:10:06.070 --> 00:10:10.450
she gives us the following with
her protagonist Laura writing

00:10:10.450 --> 00:10:12.445
to a younger woman, Marianne.

00:10:15.850 --> 00:10:18.850
"Convinced as you must be from
what I have already told you

00:10:18.850 --> 00:10:22.210
concerning Augustus and Sophia,
that there never were a happier

00:10:22.210 --> 00:10:25.540
couple, I need not,
I imagine, inform you

00:10:25.540 --> 00:10:28.180
that their union had been
contrary to the inclinations

00:10:28.180 --> 00:10:32.050
of their cruel and mercenary
parents, who had vainly

00:10:32.050 --> 00:10:34.540
endeavored, with
obstinate perseverance,

00:10:34.540 --> 00:10:36.430
to force them into a
marriage with those

00:10:36.430 --> 00:10:39.160
whom they had ever abhorred.

00:10:39.160 --> 00:10:41.740
But with an heroic fortitude
worthy to be related

00:10:41.740 --> 00:10:44.440
and admired, they had
both constantly refused

00:10:44.440 --> 00:10:48.280
to submit to such
despotic power.

00:10:48.280 --> 00:10:50.380
After having so nobly
disentangled themselves

00:10:50.380 --> 00:10:52.810
from the shackles of
parental authority

00:10:52.810 --> 00:10:55.840
by a clandestine marriage,
they were determined never

00:10:55.840 --> 00:10:58.390
to forfeit the good opinion
they had gained in the world

00:10:58.390 --> 00:11:02.560
in so doing by accepting any
proposals of reconciliation

00:11:02.560 --> 00:11:05.297
that might be offered
them by their fathers.

00:11:05.297 --> 00:11:07.380
To this farther trial of
their noble independence,

00:11:07.380 --> 00:11:09.610
however, they were
never exposed."

00:11:09.610 --> 00:11:11.410
[LAUGHTER]

00:11:12.760 --> 00:11:15.280
She further displays
her mastery of satire

00:11:15.280 --> 00:11:18.700
in The History of England
by a Partial, Prejudiced

00:11:18.700 --> 00:11:22.000
and Ignorant
Historian, in which she

00:11:22.000 --> 00:11:25.090
imitates the dry narration
of Gibbon and Goldsmith

00:11:25.090 --> 00:11:27.700
and includes a running
joke on her fidelity

00:11:27.700 --> 00:11:30.430
to Mary, Queen of Scots.

00:11:30.430 --> 00:11:34.270
Here she gives us the reign of
Edward VI, son of Henry VIII.

00:11:38.600 --> 00:11:40.430
"As this prince was
only nine years old

00:11:40.430 --> 00:11:42.290
at the time of his
father's death,

00:11:42.290 --> 00:11:45.900
he was considered by many
people as too young to govern.

00:11:45.900 --> 00:11:47.360
And the late king,
happening to be

00:11:47.360 --> 00:11:50.010
of the same opinion,
his mother's brother,

00:11:50.010 --> 00:11:52.820
the Duke of Somerset, was
chosen protector of the realm

00:11:52.820 --> 00:11:54.980
during his minority.

00:11:54.980 --> 00:11:58.040
The duke was beheaded, of
which he might with reason have

00:11:58.040 --> 00:11:59.840
been proud, had
he known that such

00:11:59.840 --> 00:12:03.190
was the death of Mary,
Queen of Scotland,

00:12:03.190 --> 00:12:05.840
but as it was impossible that
he should be conscious of what

00:12:05.840 --> 00:12:07.700
had never happened,
it does not appear

00:12:07.700 --> 00:12:09.560
that he felt
particularly delighted

00:12:09.560 --> 00:12:10.896
with the manner of it."

00:12:10.896 --> 00:12:14.630
[LAUGHTER]

00:12:14.630 --> 00:12:18.640
The use of humor to probe the
darker side of human behavior

00:12:18.640 --> 00:12:22.570
from a safe distance is
perfected in a complete novella

00:12:22.570 --> 00:12:26.740
Austen wrote, probably in
1794, when she would have been

00:12:26.740 --> 00:12:30.510
18 years old titled Lady Susan.

00:12:30.510 --> 00:12:32.830
It's in the epistolary
form and features

00:12:32.830 --> 00:12:37.690
a smart, manipulative,
almost amoral protagonist,

00:12:37.690 --> 00:12:41.470
a widow and mother who uses
charm and a thorough mastery

00:12:41.470 --> 00:12:44.410
of social conventions
to get on in the world,

00:12:44.410 --> 00:12:46.330
cheerfully disrupting
and privately

00:12:46.330 --> 00:12:48.730
laughing at the dull but
well-meaning people who

00:12:48.730 --> 00:12:50.590
come in her way.

00:12:50.590 --> 00:12:54.003
This is the story what Stillman
adopted as Love and Friendship,

00:12:54.003 --> 00:12:55.420
the film we're
screening tomorrow.

00:12:55.420 --> 00:12:59.460
And I can assure you that it's
something very delightful.

00:12:59.460 --> 00:13:02.920
The story's power derives from
the charisma of Lady Susan

00:13:02.920 --> 00:13:08.410
herself but also from the
exposure of mannered behavior

00:13:08.410 --> 00:13:11.620
as a resource that,
while ostensibly designed

00:13:11.620 --> 00:13:14.620
to convey respect
and deference, can

00:13:14.620 --> 00:13:18.730
be deployed for completely
self-serving ends.

00:13:18.730 --> 00:13:20.770
And this, I think,
is an insight that

00:13:20.770 --> 00:13:23.950
propels Jane Austen into the
world of her mature fiction.

00:13:26.840 --> 00:13:31.070
The very next year,
1795, she began

00:13:31.070 --> 00:13:34.730
to draft the novel that
would many years later become

00:13:34.730 --> 00:13:38.450
her first published work,
Sense and Sensibility.

00:13:38.450 --> 00:13:41.540
Then titled Elinor
and Marianne, it's

00:13:41.540 --> 00:13:43.910
a study, among other
things, of the value

00:13:43.910 --> 00:13:48.560
of suppressing emotion out
of consideration for others.

00:13:48.560 --> 00:13:51.800
Elinor Dashwood is the
opposite of Lady Susan

00:13:51.800 --> 00:13:56.450
in every respect but one, that
her demeanor does not reflect

00:13:56.450 --> 00:13:59.450
her true thoughts and feelings.

00:13:59.450 --> 00:14:02.750
When we step into society,
we step into a role,

00:14:02.750 --> 00:14:05.680
as Elinor knows too well.

00:14:05.680 --> 00:14:10.700
And Marianne, who can be
nothing but natural and sincere,

00:14:10.700 --> 00:14:13.130
suffers greatly in
London, where Willoughby

00:14:13.130 --> 00:14:16.820
is free to politely rebuff her.

00:14:16.820 --> 00:14:20.540
In Ang Lee's film, we
rejoice that Colonel Brandon

00:14:20.540 --> 00:14:23.990
will protect Marianne from that
knowledge of the world that

00:14:23.990 --> 00:14:29.780
might render her more
mature but less unspoilt.

00:14:29.780 --> 00:14:32.450
It's a story of two
sisters in love.

00:14:32.450 --> 00:14:37.550
And for a brief time, Jane and
Cassandra knew that happiness.

00:14:37.550 --> 00:14:40.520
In 1795, Cassandra
had been engaged

00:14:40.520 --> 00:14:44.210
to Tom Fowle, a young clergyman
with very little income,

00:14:44.210 --> 00:14:46.580
for three years.

00:14:46.580 --> 00:14:49.310
That autumn, Tom agreed
to sail to the West Indies

00:14:49.310 --> 00:14:52.760
as chaplain for a regiment
of the British militia.

00:14:52.760 --> 00:14:56.120
Upon his return,
they would marry.

00:14:56.120 --> 00:14:57.980
Cassandra spent that
Christmas holiday

00:14:57.980 --> 00:15:02.180
with the Fowle family in
Berkshire to see Tom off.

00:15:02.180 --> 00:15:05.720
And the visit is the occasion
for our first surviving letter

00:15:05.720 --> 00:15:10.250
from Jane to her sister,
dated January 1796,

00:15:10.250 --> 00:15:13.760
when Jane was just 20.

00:15:13.760 --> 00:15:16.310
With the satiric humor
that hardly disguises

00:15:16.310 --> 00:15:19.400
a sense of joyful
anticipation, Jane

00:15:19.400 --> 00:15:23.180
refers to a young
man named Tom Lefroy,

00:15:23.180 --> 00:15:25.580
an Irish cousin of
neighbors with whom

00:15:25.580 --> 00:15:27.620
the Austens were friendly.

00:15:27.620 --> 00:15:30.650
He's visiting Hampshire
on his way to London.

00:15:30.650 --> 00:15:33.380
And Jane confesses that she's
danced with him repeatedly,

00:15:33.380 --> 00:15:37.130
that Tom is being teased
about his attachment to her,

00:15:37.130 --> 00:15:40.310
and that she greatly
enjoys his company.

00:15:40.310 --> 00:15:43.250
"I'm almost afraid to tell
you how my Irish friend and I

00:15:43.250 --> 00:15:45.310
behaved," she writes.

00:15:45.310 --> 00:15:48.560
"Imagine to yourself everything
most profligate and shocking

00:15:48.560 --> 00:15:50.940
in the way of dancing and
sitting down together."

00:15:50.940 --> 00:15:53.700
[LAUGHTER]

00:15:55.550 --> 00:15:57.260
It's appropriate to
pause here and say

00:15:57.260 --> 00:16:00.230
a word about Austen's letters.

00:16:00.230 --> 00:16:03.080
She wrote them to scores
of friends and relations.

00:16:03.080 --> 00:16:06.590
But, in particular, when she
and Cassandra were separated,

00:16:06.590 --> 00:16:09.890
Jane wrote copiously and often.

00:16:09.890 --> 00:16:12.290
In a stream of
consciousness style,

00:16:12.290 --> 00:16:17.000
she records neighborhood news,
visits given and received,

00:16:17.000 --> 00:16:20.210
the appearance or dress
of this or that person,

00:16:20.210 --> 00:16:22.210
the quality of a meal.

00:16:22.210 --> 00:16:24.980
And she often works out
the cutting side of her wit

00:16:24.980 --> 00:16:28.460
as if seizing an outlet
for honest thoughts

00:16:28.460 --> 00:16:31.490
impossible to
express in company.

00:16:31.490 --> 00:16:34.700
In that sense, a picture of
her character drawn exclusively

00:16:34.700 --> 00:16:37.670
from her letters would
be woefully incomplete.

00:16:37.670 --> 00:16:40.460
It's clear that Cassandra
burned precisely

00:16:40.460 --> 00:16:46.080
the more personal missives to
protect her sister's privacy.

00:16:46.080 --> 00:16:50.250
Claire Tomalin, whose biography
of Austen I highly recommend,

00:16:50.250 --> 00:16:54.990
remarks of this
letter to Cassandra.

00:16:54.990 --> 00:16:58.410
"The fact that the manuscript
Elinor and Marianne was lying

00:16:58.410 --> 00:17:01.290
completed somewhere in
the house as she wrote

00:17:01.290 --> 00:17:04.829
gives this first letter
an added resonance.

00:17:04.829 --> 00:17:07.829
It is the only surviving
letter in which Jane is clearly

00:17:07.829 --> 00:17:11.609
writing as the heroine of
her own youthful story,

00:17:11.609 --> 00:17:16.020
living for herself the short
period of power, excitement,

00:17:16.020 --> 00:17:18.839
and adventure that might
come to a young woman

00:17:18.839 --> 00:17:21.869
when she was thinking
of choosing a husband.

00:17:21.869 --> 00:17:28.580
Just for a brief time, she is
enacting instead of imagining.

00:17:28.580 --> 00:17:32.060
We can't help knowing that
her personal story will not

00:17:32.060 --> 00:17:35.840
go in the direction she is
imagining in the letter,

00:17:35.840 --> 00:17:39.530
that, as it turned out, it
was not Tom Lefroy or anyone

00:17:39.530 --> 00:17:43.430
like him who became her
adventure but the manuscript

00:17:43.430 --> 00:17:47.560
upstairs, not marriage but art.

00:17:47.560 --> 00:17:50.990
And in her art, she made this
short period in a young woman's

00:17:50.990 --> 00:17:55.370
life carry such wit and human
understanding as few writers

00:17:55.370 --> 00:17:58.910
have managed to cram into solemn
volumes three times the size."

00:18:01.860 --> 00:18:06.710
75 years later, Tom Lefroy
told Austen's nephew

00:18:06.710 --> 00:18:11.120
that he fell in love with
Jane then with a boyish love.

00:18:11.120 --> 00:18:13.850
But he was expected
to study law and work

00:18:13.850 --> 00:18:15.750
for his family's advancement.

00:18:15.750 --> 00:18:17.810
And he could not afford
to marry a woman who

00:18:17.810 --> 00:18:19.940
wouldn't bring him a penny.

00:18:19.940 --> 00:18:22.340
He later indeed became
a judge and then

00:18:22.340 --> 00:18:25.820
Lord Chief Justice of Ireland,
amply fulfilling his family's

00:18:25.820 --> 00:18:29.160
trust and expectation.

00:18:29.160 --> 00:18:30.890
So the relations with
whom he was staying

00:18:30.890 --> 00:18:33.590
whisked him away and took care
of that he and Jane did not

00:18:33.590 --> 00:18:36.350
cross paths on his
subsequent visits.

00:18:36.350 --> 00:18:39.230
She never saw him again.

00:18:39.230 --> 00:18:43.160
Cassandra's fiancee died
of a fever in Santo Domingo

00:18:43.160 --> 00:18:45.670
in the spring of 1797.

00:18:45.670 --> 00:18:47.300
And though she was
still in her 20s,

00:18:47.300 --> 00:18:50.210
she never considered
another marriage.

00:18:50.210 --> 00:18:53.990
Jane, on the other hand, did
entertain that possibility,

00:18:53.990 --> 00:18:55.545
but we'll come to
that in its place.

00:18:58.560 --> 00:19:03.420
How would Jane Austen have
appeared to a potential suitor?

00:19:03.420 --> 00:19:06.090
The descriptions of young
men and women in her novels

00:19:06.090 --> 00:19:08.040
are not terribly detailed.

00:19:08.040 --> 00:19:12.750
She focuses on height,
the manner of movement,

00:19:12.750 --> 00:19:16.770
and the quality of expression
that can be found in the eyes.

00:19:16.770 --> 00:19:19.530
We know that Cassandra was
considered the prettier

00:19:19.530 --> 00:19:21.450
of the two sisters.

00:19:21.450 --> 00:19:25.080
Jane's brother Henry said
that had Jane been any taller,

00:19:25.080 --> 00:19:27.150
she would have exceeded
the middle height.

00:19:29.720 --> 00:19:34.070
Several relations described
her as either slender or spare.

00:19:34.070 --> 00:19:36.650
Her posture was
upright, and her step

00:19:36.650 --> 00:19:40.550
was light and firm or
quick and decisive.

00:19:40.550 --> 00:19:44.660
Her eyes were bright and her
cheeks were full of color.

00:19:44.660 --> 00:19:47.240
Notably, she is the only
one of eight siblings,

00:19:47.240 --> 00:19:49.490
excepting her
brother George, who

00:19:49.490 --> 00:19:53.060
never had a professional
portrait of any kind taken.

00:19:53.060 --> 00:19:57.050
The only image that survives
that was taken from the life

00:19:57.050 --> 00:20:00.980
is this sketch with some
watercolor drawn by her sister

00:20:00.980 --> 00:20:06.470
Cassandra, seen here, though
Austen's niece Anna, with whom

00:20:06.470 --> 00:20:09.890
she was close, later declared
that this portrait was

00:20:09.890 --> 00:20:11.900
hideously unlike her Aunt Jane.

00:20:11.900 --> 00:20:13.830
[LAUGHTER]

00:20:13.830 --> 00:20:15.720
So she remains a
bit of a mystery.

00:20:15.720 --> 00:20:19.350
And fittingly, we meet her
in her language rather than

00:20:19.350 --> 00:20:20.080
any image.

00:20:23.320 --> 00:20:26.740
In the summer of
1796, Reverend Austen

00:20:26.740 --> 00:20:29.410
made a decision,
the first of two

00:20:29.410 --> 00:20:32.140
that would have momentous
consequences for his daughter

00:20:32.140 --> 00:20:33.490
Jane.

00:20:33.490 --> 00:20:36.280
Well clear of debt and
with their sons embarked

00:20:36.280 --> 00:20:38.830
on their respective
careers, he and his wife

00:20:38.830 --> 00:20:41.170
would cease taking boarders
and running their home

00:20:41.170 --> 00:20:42.940
as a boy's school.

00:20:42.940 --> 00:20:46.150
This meant that the burden of
cooking, serving, and cleaning

00:20:46.150 --> 00:20:49.853
was greatly lessened for
the women of the house

00:20:49.853 --> 00:20:51.520
and that Jane had
more leisure to write.

00:20:54.130 --> 00:20:57.640
That autumn, she began
drafting First Impressions,

00:20:57.640 --> 00:21:00.520
the work later renamed
Pride and Prejudice.

00:21:00.520 --> 00:21:04.000
Within a year, she had
polished that to satisfaction

00:21:04.000 --> 00:21:07.090
and took up Elinor
and Marianne, revising

00:21:07.090 --> 00:21:11.260
from the epistolary style
to third person narration.

00:21:11.260 --> 00:21:16.570
And I think this effort
shows the sense of a vocation

00:21:16.570 --> 00:21:19.270
and something much
more than the desire

00:21:19.270 --> 00:21:21.880
to simply write for the
amusement of herself

00:21:21.880 --> 00:21:24.280
or her family.

00:21:24.280 --> 00:21:27.680
In 1798 and '99, she wrote
the novel later published

00:21:27.680 --> 00:21:31.420
as Northanger Abbey,
then titled Susan.

00:21:31.420 --> 00:21:34.570
And in November
1797, Jane's father,

00:21:34.570 --> 00:21:37.390
possibly without her
knowledge, sent a letter

00:21:37.390 --> 00:21:41.140
to one Thomas Cadell,
a publisher in London

00:21:41.140 --> 00:21:44.950
whose firm had printed the
works of Gibbon and Johnson.

00:21:44.950 --> 00:21:46.660
Reverend Austen
wrote that he had

00:21:46.660 --> 00:21:49.450
a manuscript for a
novel in his possession

00:21:49.450 --> 00:21:52.060
and would Mr. Cadell
consider bringing it out

00:21:52.060 --> 00:21:54.700
at the author's expense.

00:21:54.700 --> 00:21:57.220
The publisher opened
the letter, wrote

00:21:57.220 --> 00:22:00.670
"declined by return of
post" across the top,

00:22:00.670 --> 00:22:01.560
and mailed it back.

00:22:04.220 --> 00:22:05.660
The fact that
Austen's father was

00:22:05.660 --> 00:22:10.040
one of her most ardent admirers
makes it all the more poignant

00:22:10.040 --> 00:22:13.250
that his well-deserved
retirement from the parish

00:22:13.250 --> 00:22:15.350
of Steventon brought
Jane's writing

00:22:15.350 --> 00:22:20.840
life to a decisive halt. This
second and equally momentous

00:22:20.840 --> 00:22:23.960
decision was announced
to Jane and Cassandra

00:22:23.960 --> 00:22:27.380
over the Christmas
holidays of 1800.

00:22:27.380 --> 00:22:32.580
By January 1801, plans to
move to Bath were underway.

00:22:32.580 --> 00:22:35.150
Jane's piano was now sold.

00:22:35.150 --> 00:22:38.510
Her father's library
was sold off too.

00:22:38.510 --> 00:22:42.350
Her parents intended to enjoy
the crowded social calendar--

00:22:42.350 --> 00:22:45.050
theater, dances, card parties--

00:22:45.050 --> 00:22:48.020
commonly regarded as one of
the prime attractions of Bath

00:22:48.020 --> 00:22:52.220
and to take various
forays to seaside resorts.

00:22:52.220 --> 00:22:55.250
Jane and her parents removed
to temporary lodgings

00:22:55.250 --> 00:22:58.310
that spring while Cassandra
made a visit with friends

00:22:58.310 --> 00:23:00.060
before joining them.

00:23:00.060 --> 00:23:02.540
And Jane's letters
from this time

00:23:02.540 --> 00:23:06.330
reflect a struggle
with low spirits.

00:23:06.330 --> 00:23:09.630
"Another stupid party
last night," she wrote.

00:23:09.630 --> 00:23:13.350
"One card table with six people
to look over and talk nonsense

00:23:13.350 --> 00:23:15.240
to each other."

00:23:15.240 --> 00:23:19.013
Regarding their search for
a home, "when you arrive,

00:23:19.013 --> 00:23:20.430
we will at least
have the pleasure

00:23:20.430 --> 00:23:24.210
of examining some of these
putrefying houses again.

00:23:24.210 --> 00:23:27.090
They are so very desirable
in size and situation

00:23:27.090 --> 00:23:32.450
that there is some satisfaction
in spending 10 minutes within."

00:23:32.450 --> 00:23:35.870
And the note of dejection
is unmistakable.

00:23:35.870 --> 00:23:41.100
"I cannot, anyhow, continue
to find people agreeable."

00:23:41.100 --> 00:23:44.400
this from a woman who could
make a cramped setting,

00:23:44.400 --> 00:23:47.880
or a trivial card game,
or a disagreeable person

00:23:47.880 --> 00:23:50.700
bristle with
interest on the page.

00:23:50.700 --> 00:23:52.470
In her writing desk
were manuscripts

00:23:52.470 --> 00:23:56.610
for three complete novels,
but her words now dried up.

00:24:00.590 --> 00:24:05.360
That Jane was tempted by a
marriage proposal in 1802

00:24:05.360 --> 00:24:09.770
may reflect both her unhappiness
in Bath and her sense

00:24:09.770 --> 00:24:14.210
that her period of creative
fruitfulness was at an end.

00:24:14.210 --> 00:24:17.390
Harris Bigg-Wither was
the younger brother

00:24:17.390 --> 00:24:20.300
of three sisters who were
close, lifelong friends

00:24:20.300 --> 00:24:22.010
to Jane and Cassandra.

00:24:22.010 --> 00:24:26.030
He was five years Jane's junior
when he proposed to her just

00:24:26.030 --> 00:24:29.060
shortly before her
27th birthday while she

00:24:29.060 --> 00:24:31.820
was visiting for the holidays.

00:24:31.820 --> 00:24:34.820
Harris was the only son
and heir to his father's

00:24:34.820 --> 00:24:36.767
considerable estates.

00:24:36.767 --> 00:24:38.600
And as he would be
wealthy in his own right,

00:24:38.600 --> 00:24:42.620
there was no need for
any dowry on her side.

00:24:42.620 --> 00:24:45.350
Jane accepted his
proposal, and the family

00:24:45.350 --> 00:24:47.750
celebrated the good news.

00:24:47.750 --> 00:24:51.170
But by early next morning,
she had packed her bags

00:24:51.170 --> 00:24:54.470
and asked to see Harris again.

00:24:54.470 --> 00:24:56.480
We can't know what
passed between them,

00:24:56.480 --> 00:24:58.580
other than that she
withdrew her acceptance

00:24:58.580 --> 00:25:02.570
and made arrangements
to leave immediately.

00:25:02.570 --> 00:25:04.070
Did she determine
that she could not

00:25:04.070 --> 00:25:07.490
be happy in the absence
of romantic love?

00:25:07.490 --> 00:25:09.980
Did she fear the risks
associated with bringing

00:25:09.980 --> 00:25:12.050
children into the world?

00:25:12.050 --> 00:25:15.050
Was she unable to face the
prospect of giving up her

00:25:15.050 --> 00:25:17.090
writing for good?

00:25:17.090 --> 00:25:20.600
We can only speculate on the
basis of those of her heroines

00:25:20.600 --> 00:25:23.120
who turned down
offers of marriage,

00:25:23.120 --> 00:25:26.060
Elizabeth Bennet, who
will not marry Mr. Collins

00:25:26.060 --> 00:25:29.120
because, in the absence of
deep respect and affection,

00:25:29.120 --> 00:25:31.880
they will not make
one another happy;

00:25:31.880 --> 00:25:35.690
Fanny Price, who turns down
Henry Crawford because she

00:25:35.690 --> 00:25:38.690
doubts his integrity
of character;

00:25:38.690 --> 00:25:41.420
and Anne Elliott,
who refuses Charles

00:25:41.420 --> 00:25:45.200
Musgrove because her heart
still belongs to another man.

00:25:47.870 --> 00:25:53.480
In January of 1885, the Reverend
Austen died peacefully in Bath.

00:25:53.480 --> 00:25:56.660
For four years, Jane had carried
her manuscripts from place

00:25:56.660 --> 00:25:59.030
to place, packed in
her writing desk.

00:25:59.030 --> 00:26:02.420
As far as we know, she had
attempted only one fragment

00:26:02.420 --> 00:26:05.670
of a composition never pursued.

00:26:05.670 --> 00:26:08.660
True, in 1803, she
had achieved what

00:26:08.660 --> 00:26:12.410
might be called her first
professional success by selling

00:26:12.410 --> 00:26:15.290
the manuscript of
Northanger Abbey

00:26:15.290 --> 00:26:19.700
to the publisher Richard
Crosby for 10 pounds, something

00:26:19.700 --> 00:26:22.910
like $400 in our currency.

00:26:22.910 --> 00:26:25.350
But as Crosby never
printed her work,

00:26:25.350 --> 00:26:30.110
this was a somewhat discouraging
success as first successes go.

00:26:30.110 --> 00:26:33.710
And now the Austen women, Jane,
Cassandra, and their mother,

00:26:33.710 --> 00:26:36.140
were effectively homeless.

00:26:36.140 --> 00:26:39.100
They must forfeit the annuity
Reverend Austen had earned

00:26:39.100 --> 00:26:41.000
as part of his retirement.

00:26:41.000 --> 00:26:43.670
They would live on whatever
Jane's brothers together

00:26:43.670 --> 00:26:45.980
could provide them
with the interests

00:26:45.980 --> 00:26:47.750
that Mrs. Austen
and Cassandra earned

00:26:47.750 --> 00:26:49.430
on some small investments.

00:26:49.430 --> 00:26:51.530
Jane had none.

00:26:51.530 --> 00:26:54.950
They sometimes stayed near
relations in Bath but only

00:26:54.950 --> 00:26:56.600
in rented lodgings.

00:26:56.600 --> 00:27:00.920
They sometimes stayed
with friends in Hampshire

00:27:00.920 --> 00:27:03.210
and sometimes with
Jane's brother Edward

00:27:03.210 --> 00:27:07.410
at his large estate
in Kent, seen here.

00:27:07.410 --> 00:27:10.240
But they never stayed
any one place very long.

00:27:10.240 --> 00:27:13.440
And they were always
visitors or guests.

00:27:13.440 --> 00:27:16.530
This continued for
another four years,

00:27:16.530 --> 00:27:19.920
making a gap of eight years
in the career of a young woman

00:27:19.920 --> 00:27:23.530
who began to write at
12 or 14 and died at 41.

00:27:26.150 --> 00:27:29.240
Finally, in 1809,
Edward Austen, owner

00:27:29.240 --> 00:27:31.850
of a great deal of land
and property, this estate

00:27:31.850 --> 00:27:35.980
and others, realized there
were houses at his disposal

00:27:35.980 --> 00:27:38.746
and that his mother and sisters
might like to stay in one.

00:27:38.746 --> 00:27:40.084
[LAUGHTER]

00:27:41.870 --> 00:27:44.450
They chose a rather
large brick home

00:27:44.450 --> 00:27:49.250
situated at a crossroads in
Chawton village in Hampshire.

00:27:49.250 --> 00:27:51.650
They had a garden
to work at the back.

00:27:51.650 --> 00:27:54.170
Mrs. Austen was a
devoted gardener.

00:27:54.170 --> 00:27:56.600
And they spent time
making clothes and giving

00:27:56.600 --> 00:27:58.490
reading lessons to
some of the needier

00:27:58.490 --> 00:28:00.980
children in the village.

00:28:00.980 --> 00:28:04.790
Jane's life was
effectively transformed.

00:28:04.790 --> 00:28:07.130
The women agreed that Jane
would prepare the family

00:28:07.130 --> 00:28:09.230
breakfast of tea and toast.

00:28:09.230 --> 00:28:11.300
She was typically
the first to rise.

00:28:11.300 --> 00:28:13.700
But thereafter, the
cooking, gardening,

00:28:13.700 --> 00:28:15.890
and general housework
would be overseen

00:28:15.890 --> 00:28:17.500
by her sister and mother.

00:28:17.500 --> 00:28:22.060
And in moments of
leisure, Jane could write.

00:28:22.060 --> 00:28:25.660
She began immediately, took the
manuscripts out of her writing

00:28:25.660 --> 00:28:28.360
desk, and set to revision.

00:28:28.360 --> 00:28:33.760
Her lively mind, set at ease by
the security of a settled home,

00:28:33.760 --> 00:28:37.090
could once again do
with seeing nothing.

00:28:37.090 --> 00:28:40.600
And this ordinary nothing,
the nothing special

00:28:40.600 --> 00:28:43.810
of a daily routine that had
been so long denied her,

00:28:43.810 --> 00:28:46.870
could once again answer
her attentiveness

00:28:46.870 --> 00:28:50.350
with the voice of inspiration.

00:28:50.350 --> 00:28:52.960
In 1810, she sold
Sense and Sensibility

00:28:52.960 --> 00:28:56.880
and saw her first work in
print the following year,

00:28:56.880 --> 00:28:59.320
a commercial success.

00:28:59.320 --> 00:29:01.600
The achievement must
have been all the sweeter

00:29:01.600 --> 00:29:03.700
given how long she had waited.

00:29:03.700 --> 00:29:07.360
And from our perspective, it's
made bittersweet by the fact

00:29:07.360 --> 00:29:10.870
that Austen would enjoy the
full exercise of her powers

00:29:10.870 --> 00:29:13.840
for only five more years.

00:29:13.840 --> 00:29:16.060
But as an exuberant
and energetic writer,

00:29:16.060 --> 00:29:18.070
she made them count.

00:29:18.070 --> 00:29:19.870
With one commercial
success to her name,

00:29:19.870 --> 00:29:22.030
her publisher,
Thomas Egerton, was

00:29:22.030 --> 00:29:25.360
happy to purchase the copyright
to Pride and Prejudice

00:29:25.360 --> 00:29:27.940
in November of 1812.

00:29:27.940 --> 00:29:32.230
Jane describes lopping and
cropping her 1797 manuscript

00:29:32.230 --> 00:29:36.430
into the form so beloved
by all her admirers.

00:29:36.430 --> 00:29:39.190
Elizabeth Bennett
never fails to charm us

00:29:39.190 --> 00:29:41.860
with her intelligence,
self-assurance,

00:29:41.860 --> 00:29:43.480
and sense of humor.

00:29:43.480 --> 00:29:45.700
And Fitzwilliam Darcy
is a man of reserve

00:29:45.700 --> 00:29:50.350
whose warm and devoted
nature is gradually revealed.

00:29:50.350 --> 00:29:52.240
The drama turns on
that moment when

00:29:52.240 --> 00:29:56.440
Elizabeth, overly trusting
of her first impressions,

00:29:56.440 --> 00:29:59.830
is forced to revise her
estimation of Wickham,

00:29:59.830 --> 00:30:04.130
of Darcy, and most
especially of herself.

00:30:04.130 --> 00:30:06.590
But alongside that
plot line runs

00:30:06.590 --> 00:30:10.670
another and related one that
is, I think, easy to overlook.

00:30:10.670 --> 00:30:15.940
And in fact, our 1940
film version of the novel

00:30:15.940 --> 00:30:18.080
elides this.

00:30:18.080 --> 00:30:19.550
Though she's
enlightened regarding

00:30:19.550 --> 00:30:22.790
Wickham's true history
with the Darcy family,

00:30:22.790 --> 00:30:25.460
there are still depths
to Mr. Darcy's character

00:30:25.460 --> 00:30:28.070
that Elizabeth has
not yet plumbed,

00:30:28.070 --> 00:30:32.290
and so she still faces, as we
all must, a moment of decision,

00:30:32.290 --> 00:30:34.170
of faith.

00:30:34.170 --> 00:30:37.490
And it is her instinctive,
almost unthinking willingness

00:30:37.490 --> 00:30:42.020
in the novel to trust Mr. Darcy
with her sister's humiliating

00:30:42.020 --> 00:30:48.410
story that actually allows him
to rescue Lydia and the Bennet

00:30:48.410 --> 00:30:49.570
family's reputation.

00:30:52.240 --> 00:30:54.520
Jane spent the next
two years drafting

00:30:54.520 --> 00:30:59.140
her most ambitious work
yet, Mansfield Park.

00:30:59.140 --> 00:31:03.100
It is the work most provocative
of differences among her fans.

00:31:03.100 --> 00:31:08.680
And I've not yet seen a film
that I believe does it justice.

00:31:08.680 --> 00:31:12.550
Its heroine is Fanny Price,
one of nine siblings born

00:31:12.550 --> 00:31:16.540
to overwhelmed and far
from prosperous parents.

00:31:16.540 --> 00:31:19.390
But her mother's sister
has married a baronet.

00:31:19.390 --> 00:31:22.510
And the Bertram family
offered to adopt Fanny

00:31:22.510 --> 00:31:25.030
as a way of helping out.

00:31:25.030 --> 00:31:27.400
She grows into womanhood
in outwardly comfortable

00:31:27.400 --> 00:31:33.160
circumstances but remains
painfully shy, physically weak,

00:31:33.160 --> 00:31:37.730
and of a somewhat secondary
status among the Bertrams.

00:31:37.730 --> 00:31:40.180
Edmund, the kindest
of her cousins,

00:31:40.180 --> 00:31:42.730
is her only friend apart from
her brother William, with whom

00:31:42.730 --> 00:31:46.270
she corresponds devotedly.

00:31:46.270 --> 00:31:50.020
These illustrations from
a late Victorian edition

00:31:50.020 --> 00:31:52.750
might give you a sense of
Fanny's somewhat passive

00:31:52.750 --> 00:31:55.030
demeanor.

00:31:55.030 --> 00:31:57.070
The settled way of
life at Mansfield Park

00:31:57.070 --> 00:32:00.460
is pleasantly unsettled by the
arrival in their neighborhood

00:32:00.460 --> 00:32:03.610
of Henry and Mary Crawford,
a brother and sister

00:32:03.610 --> 00:32:05.560
raised in London--

00:32:05.560 --> 00:32:09.100
Mary Crawford, something of a
cross between Elizabeth Bennet

00:32:09.100 --> 00:32:15.580
and Lady Susan, Henry Crawford
a flirt who may charm the reader

00:32:15.580 --> 00:32:18.250
but cannot succeed
in charming Fanny.

00:32:18.250 --> 00:32:20.950
He hatches a plan to make
her fall in love with him

00:32:20.950 --> 00:32:24.010
and is appropriately punished
by falling in love with her

00:32:24.010 --> 00:32:28.810
himself and by surprising
both himself and the reader

00:32:28.810 --> 00:32:31.930
with his potential
for transformation.

00:32:31.930 --> 00:32:35.350
Cassandra begged
Jane to let Henry

00:32:35.350 --> 00:32:37.480
win Fanny's hand in marriage.

00:32:37.480 --> 00:32:39.550
But she had other
plans, and Fanny

00:32:39.550 --> 00:32:43.600
marries her own secret
love, Cousin Edmund.

00:32:43.600 --> 00:32:46.210
Mansfield Park features
searching debates

00:32:46.210 --> 00:32:51.220
on the morality of acting,
the value of formal prayer,

00:32:51.220 --> 00:32:54.860
and the settled versus
the mobile way of life.

00:32:54.860 --> 00:32:57.280
But I think its most
remarkable feature

00:32:57.280 --> 00:33:01.510
is that Austen sustains our
interest in a heroine who,

00:33:01.510 --> 00:33:04.600
unlike every young woman
she'd previously scripted,

00:33:04.600 --> 00:33:07.590
almost never speaks.

00:33:07.590 --> 00:33:10.590
I wonder sometimes
if Fanny's silence

00:33:10.590 --> 00:33:14.550
in the face of people and
events that swirl around her,

00:33:14.550 --> 00:33:17.850
constant reminders
of her dependence,

00:33:17.850 --> 00:33:21.480
reflects something of what
Austen herself experienced

00:33:21.480 --> 00:33:24.700
during eight years when she
seldom held a pen in her hands.

00:33:27.410 --> 00:33:31.310
Mansfield Park was
published in 1814, a year

00:33:31.310 --> 00:33:33.740
after Pride and Prejudice.

00:33:33.740 --> 00:33:36.660
As if conscious that it
would not be so well liked,

00:33:36.660 --> 00:33:40.550
Austen kept a bemused record
of the opinions expressed

00:33:40.550 --> 00:33:42.770
by friends and relations.

00:33:42.770 --> 00:33:45.560
Her professional success
was increasingly known

00:33:45.560 --> 00:33:49.250
in her family circle but was
by no means universal knowledge

00:33:49.250 --> 00:33:51.890
among her acquaintance.

00:33:51.890 --> 00:33:55.940
Of Mansfield Park, she notes,
"Mr. Egerton, the publisher,

00:33:55.940 --> 00:33:59.360
praised it for its morality
and for being so equal

00:33:59.360 --> 00:34:00.620
a composition.

00:34:00.620 --> 00:34:02.720
No weak parts.

00:34:02.720 --> 00:34:05.660
Meanwhile, a Hampshire neighbor
must have unwittingly confessed

00:34:05.660 --> 00:34:09.590
that she thought Sense
and Sensibility and Pride

00:34:09.590 --> 00:34:13.340
and Prejudice downright
nonsense but expected

00:34:13.340 --> 00:34:15.800
to like Mansfield Park
better and, having finished

00:34:15.800 --> 00:34:17.628
the first volume,
flattered herself

00:34:17.628 --> 00:34:18.920
she had got through the worst."

00:34:18.920 --> 00:34:21.680
[LAUGHTER]

00:34:23.060 --> 00:34:28.130
Between 1814 and 1815,
Austen drafted Emma,

00:34:28.130 --> 00:34:30.590
perhaps her single
greatest masterpiece,

00:34:30.590 --> 00:34:32.630
written at the
height of her powers

00:34:32.630 --> 00:34:36.739
and again from the security
of settled life in Chawton.

00:34:36.739 --> 00:34:39.949
A superficial acquaintance
with Austen's canon

00:34:39.949 --> 00:34:43.850
detects the sameness of
the courtship-driven plot.

00:34:43.850 --> 00:34:46.190
But even a cursory
reading of Emma

00:34:46.190 --> 00:34:48.800
next to its predecessor,
Mansfield Park,

00:34:48.800 --> 00:34:52.190
or its successor, Persuasion,
reveals the exuberance

00:34:52.190 --> 00:34:56.389
with which Austen
relished a new challenge.

00:34:56.389 --> 00:34:59.270
Emma is everything
Fanny Price is not.

00:34:59.270 --> 00:35:02.480
As you can see here, she's
more often in motion.

00:35:02.480 --> 00:35:04.640
She's born to wealth and power.

00:35:04.640 --> 00:35:06.710
She's talkative and extroverted.

00:35:06.710 --> 00:35:10.040
She's admired by many,
including herself.

00:35:10.040 --> 00:35:14.120
Like Elizabeth Bennet, she's
a confident young woman who

00:35:14.120 --> 00:35:16.730
must confront her own errors.

00:35:16.730 --> 00:35:19.550
But Emma's education
unfolds in a series

00:35:19.550 --> 00:35:23.060
of revelations that are
drawn out through a highly

00:35:23.060 --> 00:35:24.560
intricate plot.

00:35:24.560 --> 00:35:27.380
And she's also distinct
in that, through

00:35:27.380 --> 00:35:30.920
the masterful manipulation
of point of view,

00:35:30.920 --> 00:35:36.160
her overconfidence is
exposed to us from the start.

00:35:36.160 --> 00:35:40.480
This is what generates the
delightful humor of her story.

00:35:40.480 --> 00:35:43.360
If Elizabeth is written
something like a friend

00:35:43.360 --> 00:35:48.070
to the reader, Emma is a beloved
niece, one whose progress

00:35:48.070 --> 00:35:51.460
we chart with bemused affection
from a stance of greater

00:35:51.460 --> 00:35:52.210
experience.

00:35:55.010 --> 00:35:57.290
If we look on Emma's
progress from the vantage

00:35:57.290 --> 00:36:01.160
point of more mature
knowledge, Austen herself

00:36:01.160 --> 00:36:03.410
was growing into the
role of loving advisor

00:36:03.410 --> 00:36:05.660
to her nephews and nieces.

00:36:05.660 --> 00:36:07.730
She cautioned her
niece Fanny not

00:36:07.730 --> 00:36:11.480
to be overly charmed by
charisma or wit in a suitor

00:36:11.480 --> 00:36:14.060
but to study private character.

00:36:14.060 --> 00:36:17.540
She encouraged her niece
Anna, an aspiring writer,

00:36:17.540 --> 00:36:21.230
to value precision over
flair and to be content

00:36:21.230 --> 00:36:26.510
with a narrow focus, to find
the drama in a modest setting,

00:36:26.510 --> 00:36:31.670
and to avoid phrases like
vortex of dissipation.

00:36:31.670 --> 00:36:34.880
By early 1816, when
Emma was published,

00:36:34.880 --> 00:36:37.850
Austen was herself
composing a drama

00:36:37.850 --> 00:36:42.860
in which modesty and patience
are the central virtues.

00:36:42.860 --> 00:36:47.240
In its opening chapters, Austen
describes the chastened mindset

00:36:47.240 --> 00:36:49.040
of her aging heroine.

00:36:49.040 --> 00:36:54.650
Anne Elliott is 27 and verging
on confirmed spinsterhood.

00:36:54.650 --> 00:36:58.070
"She had been forced into
prudence in her youth.

00:36:58.070 --> 00:37:03.110
She learned romance as she
grew older, the natural sequel

00:37:03.110 --> 00:37:07.250
to an unnatural beginning."

00:37:07.250 --> 00:37:08.990
In her personal
copy of the novel,

00:37:08.990 --> 00:37:12.890
Cassandra wrote in the
margin, "Dear, dear Jane, this

00:37:12.890 --> 00:37:15.680
deserves to be written
in letters of gold,"

00:37:15.680 --> 00:37:18.830
as if these words hold a special
key to Jane Austen's mind

00:37:18.830 --> 00:37:20.770
and heart.

00:37:20.770 --> 00:37:25.520
And I think the notion of
learning romance is the reason.

00:37:25.520 --> 00:37:28.280
The belief that
happy endings emerge

00:37:28.280 --> 00:37:31.910
from the quality of attention
we bestow on outwardly

00:37:31.910 --> 00:37:35.180
undramatic people as well
as commonplace moments

00:37:35.180 --> 00:37:39.320
and settings is an insight
that explains the lasting power

00:37:39.320 --> 00:37:41.840
and lasting relevance
of Austen's fiction.

00:37:46.040 --> 00:37:50.630
Originally titled The Elliotts
and published as Persuasion,

00:37:50.630 --> 00:37:53.330
this last finished
work is shorter

00:37:53.330 --> 00:37:56.210
than Austen's earlier
novels and, in some ways,

00:37:56.210 --> 00:37:58.820
more muted in tone.

00:37:58.820 --> 00:38:02.150
She was beginning
to feel unwell.

00:38:02.150 --> 00:38:04.760
She had the disposable
income to buy back

00:38:04.760 --> 00:38:06.800
the manuscript for
Northanger Abbey

00:38:06.800 --> 00:38:09.350
from Crosby's publishing
house, but she

00:38:09.350 --> 00:38:11.570
made only minimal
revisions and set

00:38:11.570 --> 00:38:15.920
it aside, perhaps a sign
of her diminished energy.

00:38:15.920 --> 00:38:19.700
In May, she visited a
spa for refreshment--

00:38:19.700 --> 00:38:24.050
May of 1816, that is, and later
that summer, entertaining house

00:38:24.050 --> 00:38:27.050
guests while Cassandra
was away, expressed

00:38:27.050 --> 00:38:30.440
with a sense of wistfulness
that, "composition

00:38:30.440 --> 00:38:34.310
seems to me impossible with a
head full of joints of mutton

00:38:34.310 --> 00:38:37.220
and doses of rhubarb."

00:38:37.220 --> 00:38:40.580
She must have been thinking
of Sanditon, a story set in

00:38:40.580 --> 00:38:42.350
and up-and-coming
resort town that

00:38:42.350 --> 00:38:44.750
appears to be, among
other things, a satire

00:38:44.750 --> 00:38:50.070
on those who frequent spas and
suffer from imaginary illness.

00:38:50.070 --> 00:38:53.730
"Sickness is a dangerous
indulgence at my time of life,"

00:38:53.730 --> 00:38:58.380
she wrote with a chin-up
attitude to her niece Fanny.

00:38:58.380 --> 00:39:01.770
Nevertheless, she was too unwell
to draft more than 12 chapters

00:39:01.770 --> 00:39:04.600
of Sanditon.

00:39:04.600 --> 00:39:09.730
Austen's niece Caroline later
described a touching scene

00:39:09.730 --> 00:39:13.110
from around this time.

00:39:13.110 --> 00:39:17.260
"Aunt Jane used often to
lie down after dinner.

00:39:17.260 --> 00:39:20.710
My grandmother herself was
frequently on the sofa,

00:39:20.710 --> 00:39:24.130
sometimes in the afternoon,
sometimes in the evening,

00:39:24.130 --> 00:39:26.770
at no fixed period of the day.

00:39:26.770 --> 00:39:28.780
She had not bad
health for her age,

00:39:28.780 --> 00:39:31.030
and she worked often
for hours in the garden

00:39:31.030 --> 00:39:34.270
and, naturally, wanted
rest afterwards.

00:39:34.270 --> 00:39:36.590
There was only one
sofa in the room.

00:39:36.590 --> 00:39:39.490
And Aunt Jane laid
upon three chairs,

00:39:39.490 --> 00:39:41.290
which she arranged for herself.

00:39:41.290 --> 00:39:46.150
I think she had a pillow, but
it never looked comfortable.

00:39:46.150 --> 00:39:48.660
She called it her sofa.

00:39:48.660 --> 00:39:52.750
And even when the other was
unoccupied, she never took it.

00:39:52.750 --> 00:39:55.730
I often asked her how she
could like the chairs best.

00:39:55.730 --> 00:39:57.910
And I suppose I worried
her into telling me

00:39:57.910 --> 00:40:01.270
the reason of her choice,
which was that if she ever

00:40:01.270 --> 00:40:05.050
used the sofa, Grandmama
would be leaving it for her

00:40:05.050 --> 00:40:07.540
and would not lie
down, as she did now,

00:40:07.540 --> 00:40:08.920
whenever she felt inclined."

00:40:11.540 --> 00:40:14.360
I can never decide
if this reflects

00:40:14.360 --> 00:40:17.900
an excess of good manners
or a sense of distance

00:40:17.900 --> 00:40:21.050
between mother and
daughter, or perhaps those

00:40:21.050 --> 00:40:23.720
are two sides of a single coin.

00:40:23.720 --> 00:40:26.000
As it happened, as
spring advanced,

00:40:26.000 --> 00:40:28.250
Jane's letters confess
that she is increasingly

00:40:28.250 --> 00:40:30.140
confined to bed.

00:40:30.140 --> 00:40:35.330
She suffers fevers, back
pain, and a growing fatigue.

00:40:35.330 --> 00:40:37.700
After periods of
seeming convalescence,

00:40:37.700 --> 00:40:40.010
she would experience
bouts of pain that

00:40:40.010 --> 00:40:42.880
belied the notion of progress.

00:40:42.880 --> 00:40:45.100
What was her illness?

00:40:45.100 --> 00:40:48.050
For many years, the suggestion
was Addison's disease,

00:40:48.050 --> 00:40:51.580
which is a tuberculosis
of the adrenal glands.

00:40:51.580 --> 00:40:53.650
But a more recent
hypothesis points

00:40:53.650 --> 00:40:56.500
to some form of lymphoma.

00:40:56.500 --> 00:40:59.330
We'll never be sure.

00:40:59.330 --> 00:41:04.020
The letters from March and April
of 1817 brim with gratitude

00:41:04.020 --> 00:41:06.070
for the devoted
care of her family

00:41:06.070 --> 00:41:09.240
and the buoyant
expectation of improvement.

00:41:09.240 --> 00:41:12.230
It's interesting to wonder
if, in this attitude,

00:41:12.230 --> 00:41:15.780
Austen was consciously crafting
a fiction for her first

00:41:15.780 --> 00:41:20.160
and last audience, her
family circle, for in April,

00:41:20.160 --> 00:41:22.350
unbeknownst even
to Cassandra, she

00:41:22.350 --> 00:41:25.320
drew up an unwitnessed will.

00:41:25.320 --> 00:41:28.740
A month later, the sisters
took lodgings in Winchester

00:41:28.740 --> 00:41:31.710
to consult an experienced
physician there.

00:41:31.710 --> 00:41:37.770
By June, Jane was apparently too
weak to take up her pen at all.

00:41:37.770 --> 00:41:41.320
But words were her
connection with life.

00:41:41.320 --> 00:41:44.490
On July 15, she
dictated to Cassandra

00:41:44.490 --> 00:41:47.400
a poem of 24 lines
on the subject

00:41:47.400 --> 00:41:50.280
of the annual
Winchester horse races.

00:41:50.280 --> 00:41:53.850
Remarkable how she continued
to turn her attention

00:41:53.850 --> 00:41:57.300
outward to the life around her.

00:41:57.300 --> 00:42:00.600
And early on the morning of
July 18, she died with her head

00:42:00.600 --> 00:42:03.330
in Cassandra's lap.

00:42:03.330 --> 00:42:08.820
Her last surviving
letter to a nephew

00:42:08.820 --> 00:42:11.220
concludes with a
poignant reflection

00:42:11.220 --> 00:42:14.340
of what this keen
observer saw and felt when

00:42:14.340 --> 00:42:18.030
she turned her eyes on herself.

00:42:18.030 --> 00:42:20.610
"God bless you, my dear Edward.

00:42:20.610 --> 00:42:23.040
If ever you are ill,
may you be as tenderly

00:42:23.040 --> 00:42:25.170
nursed as I have been.

00:42:25.170 --> 00:42:27.690
May the same blessed
alleviations of anxious,

00:42:27.690 --> 00:42:30.440
sympathizing friends be yours.

00:42:30.440 --> 00:42:33.480
And may you possess,
as I daresay you will,

00:42:33.480 --> 00:42:37.020
the greatest blessing of all in
the consciousness of not being

00:42:37.020 --> 00:42:39.480
unworthy of their love.

00:42:39.480 --> 00:42:41.820
I could not feel this.

00:42:41.820 --> 00:42:43.628
Your very affectionate
aunt, JA."

00:42:46.560 --> 00:42:49.980
She was buried in
Winchester Cathedral,

00:42:49.980 --> 00:42:53.130
the place marked with
a black marble plaque

00:42:53.130 --> 00:42:56.340
and an inscription that cites
the extraordinary endowments

00:42:56.340 --> 00:43:00.240
of her mind as just
one of many virtues.

00:43:00.240 --> 00:43:02.460
With Cassandra's
help, Henry composed

00:43:02.460 --> 00:43:05.580
a brief biographical
notice for their sister

00:43:05.580 --> 00:43:07.500
and arranged for
Northanger Abbey

00:43:07.500 --> 00:43:12.030
to be published with Persuasion
by the end of the year.

00:43:12.030 --> 00:43:15.570
The pairing is odd but also apt.

00:43:15.570 --> 00:43:19.560
Persuasion, her last
completed novel, and featuring

00:43:19.560 --> 00:43:22.710
a heroine subdued by
painful experience,

00:43:22.710 --> 00:43:25.200
alongside Northanger
Abbey, scarcely

00:43:25.200 --> 00:43:27.090
touched since Austen
had drafted it

00:43:27.090 --> 00:43:30.960
20 years earlier with her most
naive heroine and certainly

00:43:30.960 --> 00:43:33.850
her most ebullient narrator.

00:43:33.850 --> 00:43:36.420
The comparison shows us
that Austen never wrote

00:43:36.420 --> 00:43:40.800
half-heartedly, never wrote with
less than mastery of her craft,

00:43:40.800 --> 00:43:44.340
and never stopped
changing and growing.

00:43:44.340 --> 00:43:48.300
50 years later, the nephew
to whom Austen addressed

00:43:48.300 --> 00:43:51.990
her last letter, James
Edward Austen Lee,

00:43:51.990 --> 00:43:53.970
would compose a more
detailed memoir,

00:43:53.970 --> 00:43:57.060
which seems to have catalyzed
interest in her work.

00:43:57.060 --> 00:43:58.980
Both sales and
critical commentary

00:43:58.980 --> 00:44:02.070
were modest before the
turn of the century.

00:44:02.070 --> 00:44:04.830
Her popularity with
ordinary readers,

00:44:04.830 --> 00:44:07.050
as opposed to fellow
novelists, was

00:44:07.050 --> 00:44:09.690
boosted by the emergence
of paperback editions

00:44:09.690 --> 00:44:12.090
in the mid-20th century.

00:44:12.090 --> 00:44:14.070
And since then, she
has proved herself

00:44:14.070 --> 00:44:17.160
worthy of a very rare
honor, an assured

00:44:17.160 --> 00:44:20.850
place in the canon of
great books in the academy

00:44:20.850 --> 00:44:22.980
and the lasting
devotion of those

00:44:22.980 --> 00:44:26.430
who read only for pleasure.

00:44:26.430 --> 00:44:29.160
In the words of Northanger
Abbey, "And what

00:44:29.160 --> 00:44:31.020
are you reading, miss?

00:44:31.020 --> 00:44:34.020
Oh, it is only a novel,
replies the young lady,

00:44:34.020 --> 00:44:36.660
while she lays down her book
with affected indifference

00:44:36.660 --> 00:44:38.850
or momentary shame.

00:44:38.850 --> 00:44:44.610
It is only Cecilia, or Camilla,
or Belinda, or, in short,

00:44:44.610 --> 00:44:48.030
only some work in which the
greatest powers of the mind

00:44:48.030 --> 00:44:50.610
are displayed, in which
the most thorough knowledge

00:44:50.610 --> 00:44:54.810
of human nature, the happiest
delineation of its varieties,

00:44:54.810 --> 00:44:57.810
the liveliest effusions
of wit and humor

00:44:57.810 --> 00:45:03.220
are conveyed to the world in
the best chosen language."

00:45:03.220 --> 00:45:06.400
Given her current fame,
another adaptation

00:45:06.400 --> 00:45:09.430
of Sense and Sensibility
or Emma might

00:45:09.430 --> 00:45:12.170
seem ordinary, nothing special.

00:45:12.170 --> 00:45:14.590
What are your plans this
evening, you might be asked.

00:45:14.590 --> 00:45:19.270
Oh, I'm only attending
the Jane Austen CCA.

00:45:19.270 --> 00:45:21.850
I'm confident, however,
that those of us

00:45:21.850 --> 00:45:23.710
who take a closer
look will continue

00:45:23.710 --> 00:45:28.960
to prize a quiet exuberance that
belongs to only Jane Austen.

00:45:28.960 --> 00:45:29.843
Thank you.

00:45:29.843 --> 00:45:34.280
[APPLAUSE]

00:45:43.154 --> 00:45:45.220
SPEAKER: Thank you, Dr. Murphy.

00:45:45.220 --> 00:45:47.480
We now have time for some
Q&amp;A. If you have a question,

00:45:47.480 --> 00:45:49.980
please make your way to one of
the microphones in the aisle.

00:46:06.606 --> 00:46:09.000
AUDIENCE: Hi, Dr. Murphy.

00:46:09.000 --> 00:46:11.730
I was just curious
if we have access

00:46:11.730 --> 00:46:15.030
to the original draft
of Elinor and Marianne,

00:46:15.030 --> 00:46:18.090
and if we see any significant
differences between that draft

00:46:18.090 --> 00:46:20.190
and Sense and Sensibility,
which was written

00:46:20.190 --> 00:46:22.572
after all of her heartache.

00:46:22.572 --> 00:46:26.330
LORRAINE MURPHY: We do not have
access to that first draft.

00:46:26.330 --> 00:46:31.460
And I believe it's on the basis
mainly of internal evidence

00:46:31.460 --> 00:46:33.840
in the finished Sense
and Sensibility,

00:46:33.840 --> 00:46:40.370
although I think also from
one or two brief remarks

00:46:40.370 --> 00:46:42.470
in her letters
that we are pretty

00:46:42.470 --> 00:46:45.320
confident it was originally
in the epistolary style

00:46:45.320 --> 00:46:46.400
and then revised.

00:46:46.400 --> 00:46:49.310
But we do not have that draft.

00:46:49.310 --> 00:46:50.930
Of her published
novels, her finished

00:46:50.930 --> 00:46:54.740
novels, the only
manuscript that survives

00:46:54.740 --> 00:46:59.660
is interestingly, the concluding
two chapters of Persuasion,

00:46:59.660 --> 00:47:01.610
which she canceled and rewrote.

00:47:01.610 --> 00:47:04.490
So what we have is
what she threw out.

00:47:04.490 --> 00:47:06.630
And it's quite
interesting to see

00:47:06.630 --> 00:47:07.880
what she wrote the first time.

00:47:07.880 --> 00:47:11.527
But of her finished work, that's
the only manuscript in her hand

00:47:11.527 --> 00:47:12.110
that survives.

00:47:15.541 --> 00:47:16.410
AUDIENCE: Hi.

00:47:16.410 --> 00:47:19.500
So in recent years, some
criticisms of the economist

00:47:19.500 --> 00:47:22.160
Adam Smith have been that when
he wrote Wealth of Nations,

00:47:22.160 --> 00:47:24.240
he had this maidservant who was
literally taking care of him

00:47:24.240 --> 00:47:25.020
the entire time.

00:47:25.020 --> 00:47:27.687
So of course he's going to write
this incredible economic theory

00:47:27.687 --> 00:47:30.390
about capitalism because, well,
he doesn't have to do anything.

00:47:30.390 --> 00:47:32.680
At least reflecting on the
quote, like "a mind lively

00:47:32.680 --> 00:47:34.890
and at ease can do
with seeing nothing,"

00:47:34.890 --> 00:47:36.600
could you see future
literary criticisms

00:47:36.600 --> 00:47:39.360
of Jane Austen appearing
basically because, as you said,

00:47:39.360 --> 00:47:41.233
when she was at the
peak of her powers,

00:47:41.233 --> 00:47:42.900
she was allowed to
write the entire day,

00:47:42.900 --> 00:47:44.650
and it didn't really
seem to be that there

00:47:44.650 --> 00:47:46.800
is much of a struggle or
intersectional narrative

00:47:46.800 --> 00:47:50.552
being applied towards
her life at that time.

00:47:50.552 --> 00:47:52.510
LORRAINE MURPHY: If I
understand your question,

00:47:52.510 --> 00:47:55.975
it's concerning future novelists
or concerning Austen herself?

00:47:55.975 --> 00:47:57.600
AUDIENCE: So concerning
Austen herself,

00:47:57.600 --> 00:48:00.310
could you see people criticizing
her currently or in the future

00:48:00.310 --> 00:48:01.727
because when she
was writing, that

00:48:01.727 --> 00:48:03.380
was kind of all she had to do?

00:48:03.380 --> 00:48:04.920
Of course, she's going to
produce all these works.

00:48:04.920 --> 00:48:07.170
But was there any real
struggle involved at that time?

00:48:11.828 --> 00:48:13.940
LORRAINE MURPHY: You see
that critique of her work

00:48:13.940 --> 00:48:18.080
sometimes, of her fiction,
where you have a way of life.

00:48:18.080 --> 00:48:20.210
Mrs. Bennet boasts
her daughters have

00:48:20.210 --> 00:48:25.010
nothing to do in the kitchen,
that that's all taken care of.

00:48:25.010 --> 00:48:27.020
When Austen and her
sister and mother

00:48:27.020 --> 00:48:28.490
lived at Chawton
Cottage, they did

00:48:28.490 --> 00:48:30.680
have some help in the
kitchen, but that did not

00:48:30.680 --> 00:48:34.760
mean that they were uninvolved.

00:48:34.760 --> 00:48:38.450
But I mean, Jane Austen
grew up in a family

00:48:38.450 --> 00:48:42.500
where she was accustomed
to working and making

00:48:42.500 --> 00:48:43.670
a contribution.

00:48:43.670 --> 00:48:45.530
Everyone with an
able pair of hands

00:48:45.530 --> 00:48:48.470
was accustomed to working
and making a contribution.

00:48:48.470 --> 00:48:51.140
And she and her
sister and mother

00:48:51.140 --> 00:48:54.530
worked something out
where they agreed

00:48:54.530 --> 00:48:57.500
to shoulder a
burden with a loving

00:48:57.500 --> 00:49:01.700
sense of Jane's vocation.

00:49:01.700 --> 00:49:06.590
So she certainly was not
distant from the reality

00:49:06.590 --> 00:49:09.650
of labor and the way
that labor supports

00:49:09.650 --> 00:49:13.730
leisure and creativity.

00:49:13.730 --> 00:49:17.210
In her will, she left
everything to Cassandra

00:49:17.210 --> 00:49:22.160
except 50 pounds to her brother
Henry, whose bank had failed,

00:49:22.160 --> 00:49:27.980
and 50 pounds to a servant of
a sister-in-law, for whom she

00:49:27.980 --> 00:49:29.360
had a friendship.

00:49:29.360 --> 00:49:32.990
She maintained an ongoing
correspondence, a very friendly

00:49:32.990 --> 00:49:35.000
correspondence
with the woman who

00:49:35.000 --> 00:49:39.350
was the governess at her
brother Edward's home in Kent.

00:49:39.350 --> 00:49:43.370
So she seems to have had a great
sympathy for working women,

00:49:43.370 --> 00:49:48.800
and I think she was
closer to that reality

00:49:48.800 --> 00:49:51.830
than we realize if we only
read her published work.

00:49:55.478 --> 00:49:58.100
AUDIENCE: I know
you said that there

00:49:58.100 --> 00:50:01.250
weren't any satisfactory
versions of Mansfield

00:50:01.250 --> 00:50:02.780
Park on film.

00:50:02.780 --> 00:50:03.290
I'm curious.

00:50:03.290 --> 00:50:04.332
Why do you think that is?

00:50:04.332 --> 00:50:07.960
Is there something about it
that's difficult to adapt?

00:50:07.960 --> 00:50:11.060
LORRAINE MURPHY: I think so.

00:50:11.060 --> 00:50:13.370
One thing about Austen
that is very easy to adapt

00:50:13.370 --> 00:50:14.780
is her dialogue.

00:50:14.780 --> 00:50:16.400
She writes wonderful dialogue.

00:50:16.400 --> 00:50:18.440
And she has a wonderful
ear for voice,

00:50:18.440 --> 00:50:22.850
and for those little inflections
of mannerisms of speech

00:50:22.850 --> 00:50:25.520
that gives such a lively
sense of character.

00:50:25.520 --> 00:50:27.980
So in a lot of film
adaptations, you'll

00:50:27.980 --> 00:50:29.930
find the dialogue
almost word for word

00:50:29.930 --> 00:50:32.090
what you'll find in the novels.

00:50:32.090 --> 00:50:36.350
But as I said, Fanny
Price almost never speaks.

00:50:36.350 --> 00:50:38.337
And it's difficult,
I think, on film--

00:50:38.337 --> 00:50:39.920
not impossible,
certainly, but I think

00:50:39.920 --> 00:50:43.100
it's difficult to give a
sense of the drama going

00:50:43.100 --> 00:50:48.700
on in the mind with
so little being said.

00:50:48.700 --> 00:50:52.700
And in some ways, the
liveliest adaptation

00:50:52.700 --> 00:50:58.400
of Mansfield Park that I've seen
makes Fanny Price an entirely

00:50:58.400 --> 00:50:59.750
different character.

00:50:59.750 --> 00:51:02.630
And the moviemakers
seem to have decided

00:51:02.630 --> 00:51:05.810
to replace Fanny Price with
a picture of the young Jane

00:51:05.810 --> 00:51:09.470
Austen, which is interesting,
but it's not Fanny Price.

00:51:09.470 --> 00:51:14.390
So her silence and her
passivity, her weakness--

00:51:14.390 --> 00:51:16.310
even when she's
pushed or pressed,

00:51:16.310 --> 00:51:18.380
she often doesn't
wish to respond.

00:51:18.380 --> 00:51:21.470
And even for readers
of the novel,

00:51:21.470 --> 00:51:23.450
that can feel frustrating.

00:51:23.450 --> 00:51:27.187
So translating that to film is
maybe especially frustrating.

00:51:27.187 --> 00:51:28.020
AUDIENCE: Thank you.

00:51:28.020 --> 00:51:29.490
LORRAINE MURPHY: You're welcome.

00:51:29.490 --> 00:51:32.020
AUDIENCE: Hi, thank
you for your talk.

00:51:32.020 --> 00:51:35.700
I was just wondering, how common
was it at that time for women

00:51:35.700 --> 00:51:37.440
to be authors?

00:51:37.440 --> 00:51:39.090
And what kind of
obstacles did that

00:51:39.090 --> 00:51:40.875
present Austen in her career?

00:51:43.428 --> 00:51:44.470
LORRAINE MURPHY: It was--

00:51:44.470 --> 00:51:47.080
I don't know if you'd call
it common but common enough

00:51:47.080 --> 00:51:49.690
that Austen was familiar with
the published work, certainly

00:51:49.690 --> 00:51:52.150
of other female novelists.

00:51:52.150 --> 00:51:56.330
The obstacle was leisure.

00:51:56.330 --> 00:51:59.260
So even if Austen had, let's
say, married Harris Bigg-Wither

00:51:59.260 --> 00:52:02.400
and become quite a wealthy
woman living a comfortable life,

00:52:02.400 --> 00:52:04.420
her duties as the
head of the household

00:52:04.420 --> 00:52:06.520
would probably
have prevented her

00:52:06.520 --> 00:52:09.680
from having that time to sit
down and draft and revise.

00:52:14.500 --> 00:52:16.900
Women often didn't publish
under their own names.

00:52:16.900 --> 00:52:19.720
So none of Austen's work was
published in her lifetime

00:52:19.720 --> 00:52:21.250
under her name.

00:52:21.250 --> 00:52:22.930
But that didn't mean
that women weren't

00:52:22.930 --> 00:52:26.820
interested in professional
success and the independence

00:52:26.820 --> 00:52:28.100
that that could provide.

00:52:28.100 --> 00:52:31.720
So there were models,
but they were not many.

00:52:31.720 --> 00:52:34.570
And as you could see, Austen had
to sort of forge a way of life

00:52:34.570 --> 00:52:37.395
that would support her work.

00:52:37.395 --> 00:52:39.020
And that was sometimes
difficult to do.

00:52:41.840 --> 00:52:44.260
AUDIENCE: In
previous lectures, it

00:52:44.260 --> 00:52:54.250
had been said that when
she was writing her novels,

00:52:54.250 --> 00:52:56.920
she took for granted
her social class

00:52:56.920 --> 00:53:03.450
and that the novels weren't
necessarily to depict that.

00:53:03.450 --> 00:53:07.810
Would you say that that is an
accurate description of how

00:53:07.810 --> 00:53:10.250
she wrote in regards
to social class,

00:53:10.250 --> 00:53:13.270
especially considering
the fact that, at least it

00:53:13.270 --> 00:53:16.450
seems like in her
younger years and then

00:53:16.450 --> 00:53:19.720
after her father's death,
her social status wasn't

00:53:19.720 --> 00:53:20.860
necessarily secure?

00:53:23.704 --> 00:53:26.730
LORRAINE MURPHY: I think that
she took the class system,

00:53:26.730 --> 00:53:29.640
if we want to call
it that, for granted,

00:53:29.640 --> 00:53:32.720
as has been said
a couple of times.

00:53:32.720 --> 00:53:36.780
And I don't think she was
setting out to reform it

00:53:36.780 --> 00:53:42.480
or to question it in
any kind of radical way.

00:53:46.605 --> 00:53:48.480
And I think sometimes,
it's easy to overlook.

00:53:48.480 --> 00:53:57.060
She wrote about a
social way of life

00:53:57.060 --> 00:54:00.970
that was a degree or
two above her own.

00:54:00.970 --> 00:54:03.480
And it's a way of life she
would have been familiar with,

00:54:03.480 --> 00:54:06.570
for example, at her
brother Edward's home

00:54:06.570 --> 00:54:09.060
or at Harris Bigg-Withers' home.

00:54:09.060 --> 00:54:11.550
She moved in a circle
where this way of life

00:54:11.550 --> 00:54:13.980
could be known to her and
could become familiar to her,

00:54:13.980 --> 00:54:15.750
but it wasn't her way of life.

00:54:18.851 --> 00:54:21.540
And I think that
if she chooses not

00:54:21.540 --> 00:54:26.550
to make that a central tension
in her novels, the difference

00:54:26.550 --> 00:54:28.620
between what a woman
like Jane Austen

00:54:28.620 --> 00:54:31.620
has to do with her duties
and daily responsibilities

00:54:31.620 --> 00:54:33.940
versus what a woman like
Elizabeth Bennet has to do.

00:54:33.940 --> 00:54:37.620
It's a question that, in
some sense, doesn't come up

00:54:37.620 --> 00:54:45.720
or comes up in subtle, quiet
ways that can be probed

00:54:45.720 --> 00:54:48.670
but that don't really
cry out for attention.

00:54:48.670 --> 00:54:50.550
The one novel where
she does take,

00:54:50.550 --> 00:54:52.890
I think, a closer and
maybe more critical

00:54:52.890 --> 00:54:57.330
look at England's social system
is her last work, Persuasion.

00:54:57.330 --> 00:54:59.130
And it's interesting
to wonder what

00:54:59.130 --> 00:55:03.270
she might have done in Sanditon,
but we can't be too sure.

00:55:03.270 --> 00:55:04.740
But in Persuasion,
you have a kind

00:55:04.740 --> 00:55:08.940
of dialogue between
the landed gentry,

00:55:08.940 --> 00:55:11.910
as exemplified by Sir
Walter Elliot, who

00:55:11.910 --> 00:55:19.870
is a really comical but
sad failure of a baronet,

00:55:19.870 --> 00:55:21.630
and the navy.

00:55:21.630 --> 00:55:24.450
And Austen loved the navy, and
was very loyal to the navy,

00:55:24.450 --> 00:55:28.860
and very proud of her brothers
who were naval officers.

00:55:28.860 --> 00:55:33.110
And there you have a sort
of merit-based social system

00:55:33.110 --> 00:55:34.770
that, in Persuasion,
Austen seems

00:55:34.770 --> 00:55:37.260
to find very refreshing
and positive.

00:55:37.260 --> 00:55:40.860
So I wouldn't say
that social critique

00:55:40.860 --> 00:55:42.600
is absent from her work.

00:55:42.600 --> 00:55:44.670
But it's muted.

00:55:44.670 --> 00:55:51.090
And I certainly
think that she's not

00:55:51.090 --> 00:55:53.550
a thoroughgoing
radical egalitarian.

00:55:53.550 --> 00:55:58.680
She knows that roles can
be, while limiting, also

00:55:58.680 --> 00:56:02.970
very supportive of the
relationships that we establish

00:56:02.970 --> 00:56:05.095
and the work that we do,
if you like, in society.

00:56:08.378 --> 00:56:09.390
AUDIENCE: Hi.

00:56:09.390 --> 00:56:12.150
So you mentioned that Pride
and Prejudice was originally

00:56:12.150 --> 00:56:13.458
titled First Impressions.

00:56:13.458 --> 00:56:15.750
So I was wondering if you
could speak a little bit more

00:56:15.750 --> 00:56:16.440
on that.

00:56:16.440 --> 00:56:20.070
Why was the reason for
the change in title?

00:56:20.070 --> 00:56:21.090
And who did this?

00:56:21.090 --> 00:56:25.854
And then why Pride and Prejudice
versus First Impressions?

00:56:29.640 --> 00:56:31.910
LORRAINE MURPHY: I'm
not 100% sure on this,

00:56:31.910 --> 00:56:34.540
but I think that her
publisher suggested

00:56:34.540 --> 00:56:36.170
the title Pride and Prejudice.

00:56:36.170 --> 00:56:39.390
And I think that was
based on the style.

00:56:39.390 --> 00:56:41.590
It was doing well at the time.

00:56:41.590 --> 00:56:43.330
Austen herself does
not seem to have

00:56:43.330 --> 00:56:44.710
been a great writer of titles.

00:56:44.710 --> 00:56:47.050
And her siblings changed
the titles she had in mind

00:56:47.050 --> 00:56:49.160
as she drafted
things with titles.

00:56:49.160 --> 00:56:50.380
She just used the name.

00:56:50.380 --> 00:56:54.994
The Elliots, Susan, and Emma.

00:56:54.994 --> 00:56:56.962
[LAUGHTER]

00:56:58.440 --> 00:57:00.240
Why First Impressions?

00:57:00.240 --> 00:57:04.320
I think because both
Darcy and Elizabeth

00:57:04.320 --> 00:57:07.680
Bennet are too reliant on
their first impressions.

00:57:07.680 --> 00:57:18.040
And Austen was always very
interested in a society--

00:57:18.040 --> 00:57:20.410
and this builds a little
bit on the last question--

00:57:20.410 --> 00:57:23.890
where mannered behavior
was very important,

00:57:23.890 --> 00:57:26.650
and there was a kind of
decorum and structure

00:57:26.650 --> 00:57:29.400
to what you could say
and do in society.

00:57:29.400 --> 00:57:31.810
It can be difficult to
read between the lines.

00:57:39.870 --> 00:57:42.870
You have to make a judgment
based on what people say and do

00:57:42.870 --> 00:57:43.590
in society.

00:57:43.590 --> 00:57:45.865
And often, two
people who are going

00:57:45.865 --> 00:57:47.490
to be engaged to be
married would never

00:57:47.490 --> 00:57:48.720
have been alone together.

00:57:48.720 --> 00:57:50.670
They would have seen
each other entirely

00:57:50.670 --> 00:57:53.430
in social and socially
controlled settings.

00:57:53.430 --> 00:57:58.470
So the importance of looking
past first impressions

00:57:58.470 --> 00:58:02.445
is, I think, what she's really
driving at in that novel.

00:58:02.445 --> 00:58:05.875
SPEAKER: We have time
for one more question.

00:58:05.875 --> 00:58:08.750
AUDIENCE: Thank you for your
time tonight, Dr. Murphy.

00:58:08.750 --> 00:58:10.515
So from your
discussion, it seems

00:58:10.515 --> 00:58:12.390
that Austen had a really
special relationship

00:58:12.390 --> 00:58:15.480
with several members of her
family, especially her sister.

00:58:15.480 --> 00:58:18.378
And just considering that
there are several sister pairs

00:58:18.378 --> 00:58:20.670
and close female friendships
in her books, can you just

00:58:20.670 --> 00:58:23.460
talked a little bit about
how her relationship

00:58:23.460 --> 00:58:25.830
with her sister might have
impacted those relationships

00:58:25.830 --> 00:58:26.730
in the books.

00:58:26.730 --> 00:58:30.000
LORRAINE MURPHY: They
were very, very close.

00:58:30.000 --> 00:58:32.460
As I said, when Cassandra
was away, Jane had to write.

00:58:32.460 --> 00:58:34.730
She had to put down her
thoughts to Cassandra.

00:58:34.730 --> 00:58:36.480
You can see that in
the comment her mother

00:58:36.480 --> 00:58:39.900
made when they were girls,
Jane's devotion to Cassandra.

00:58:44.310 --> 00:58:46.170
I think the fact
that they had both

00:58:46.170 --> 00:58:52.380
known the sadness of perhaps
anticipating marriage--

00:58:52.380 --> 00:58:54.650
certainly, Cassandra had
anticipated a marriage.

00:58:54.650 --> 00:58:57.330
And Jane, I think, had
at least begun to do so.

00:58:57.330 --> 00:59:04.450
And letting go of that must
have bound them closer together.

00:59:04.450 --> 00:59:10.440
And by the time Jane died,
they would have anticipated

00:59:10.440 --> 00:59:12.570
growing old together.

00:59:12.570 --> 00:59:21.810
And this was-- as I say,
it's a social world where

00:59:21.810 --> 00:59:27.450
the behavior at a card party, or
in a sitting room, or at a meal

00:59:27.450 --> 00:59:30.960
has a kind of script to it.

00:59:30.960 --> 00:59:32.430
And the number of
people with whom

00:59:32.430 --> 00:59:36.720
you would share your
unvarnished, from the heart

00:59:36.720 --> 00:59:40.080
emotions and feelings and
words would be very few.

00:59:40.080 --> 00:59:42.930
And Cassandra was, I think,
that person for Jane,

00:59:42.930 --> 00:59:45.345
and I'm sure Jane was
that person for Cassandra.

00:59:45.345 --> 00:59:47.310
And sibling
relationships in general

00:59:47.310 --> 00:59:50.190
are prized in her novels.

00:59:50.190 --> 00:59:52.740
Again, we tend to think of them
as the novels about courtship

00:59:52.740 --> 00:59:56.940
and marriage, but they're
really novels about societies

00:59:56.940 --> 00:59:57.720
and communities.

00:59:57.720 --> 01:00:00.870
And siblings are
our first society.

01:00:00.870 --> 01:00:02.775
Jane loved that.

01:00:02.775 --> 01:00:05.130
SPEAKER: Please join me
in thanking Dr. Murphy.

01:00:05.130 --> 01:00:08.480
[APPLAUSE]

